928244535389e-07,-2.861018856492592e-06,-0.04803461581468582,-4.291525328881107e-06,-0.0003268184664193541,-0.012636237777769566,-3.099436753473128e-06,-2.145764938177308e-06,-0.08016818016767502,0.0,-0.0009026029147207737,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,0.0,0.0,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-1.5497195136049413e-06,-5.006777428206988e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,37175,279,4771,315,26107,59,37018,45340,61,18,6558,61,17,37892,15170,21,87,61,17,12,24,87,92,76466,10061,400,64,3,387,279,1372,315,19766,304,279,4771,11,400,65,3,387,279,1372,315,12140,438,88,1307,6295,11,400,66,3,387,279,1372,315,16202,96254,6295,11,323,400,67,3,387,279,1372,315,1508,36426,96254,6295,13,7379,400,64,10,17,65,10,18,66,10,19,67,12947,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nConsider the graph of $$\\frac{x^3-x^2+x}{6x^2-9x}.$$Let $a$ be the number of holes in the graph, $b$ be the number of vertical asympotes, $c$ be the number of horizontal asymptotes, and $d$ be the number of oblique asymptotes. Find $a+2b+3c+4d$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"95c905b59d62f43d9d3fe7dba9df18ea5eefbbfa6be9a253d7b85e1009e8fecc"},"seconds":65.4245936870575,"seed":56619237533513647,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the given problem, we need to analyze the graph of the function \\(\\frac{x^3 - x^2 + x}{6x^2 - 9x}\\). We will find the number of holes, vertical asymptotes, horizontal asymptotes, and oblique asymptotes to determine the values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(c\\), and \\(d\\).\n\nFirst, let's simplify the given function:\n\\[\nf(x) = \\frac{x^3 - x^2 + x}{6x^2 - 9x}\n\\]\n\nFactor the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\nx^3 - x^2 + x = x(x^2 - x + 1)\n\\]\n\\[\n6x^2 - 9x = 3x(2x - 3)\n\\]\n\nThus, the function becomes:\n\\[\nf(x) = \\frac{x(x^2 - x + 1)}{3x(2x - 3)}\n\\]\n\nWe can cancel the common factor of \\(x\\) from the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\nf(x) = \\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{3(2x - 3)} \\quad \\text{for} \\quad x \\neq 0\n\\]\n\n### Holes\nThe original function had a common factor of \\(x\\) in the numerator and the denominator, which was canceled. This indicates a hole at \\(x = 0\\).\n\nThus, \\(a = 1\\).\n\n### Vertical Asymptotes\nVertical asymptotes occur where the denominator of the simplified function is zero, and the numerator is non-zero. Set the denominator \\(3(2x - 3) = 0\\):\n\\[\n2x - 3 = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, there is a vertical asymptote at \\(x = \\frac{3}{2}\\).\n\nThus, \\(b = 1\\).\n\n### Horizontal Asymptotes\nTo find horizontal asymptotes, we compare the degrees of the numerator and the denominator of the simplified function \\(\\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{3(2x - 3)}\\).\n\nThe degree of the numerator \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) is 2.\nThe degree of the denominator \\(3(2x - 3)\\) is 1.\n\nSince the degree of the numerator is greater than the degree of the denominator, there is no horizontal asymptote.\n\nThus, \\(c = 0\\).\n\n### Oblique Asymptotes\nOblique asymptotes occur when the degree of the numerator is exactly one more than the degree of the denominator. Here, the degree of the numerator (2) is exactly one more than the degree of the denominator (1).\n\nTo find the oblique asymptote, we perform polynomial long division of \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) by \\(6x - 9\\).\n\nDivide \\(x^2\\) by \\(6x\\) to get \\(\\frac{x}{6}\\).\n\nMultiply \\(\\frac{x}{6}\\) by \\(6x - 9\\) to get \\(x^2 - \\frac{9x}{6} = x^2 - 1.5x\\).\n\nSubtract \\(x^2 - 1.5x\\) from \\(x^2 - x + 1\\):\n\\[\n(x^2 - x + 1) - (x^2 - 1.5x) = 0.5x + 1\n\\]\n\nDivide \\(0.5x\\) by \\(6x\\) to get \\(\\frac{0.5}{6} = \\frac{1}{12}\\).\n\nMultiply \\(\\frac{1}{12}\\) by \\(6x - 9\\) to get \\(0.5x - 0.75\\).\n\nSubtract \\(0.5x - 0.75\\) from \\(0.5x + 1\\):\n\\[\n(0.5x + 1) - (0.5x - 0.75) = 1.75\n\\]\n\nThus, the quotient is \\(\\frac{x}{6} + \\frac{1}{12}\\), and the remainder is 1.75. Therefore, the oblique asymptote is:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{x}{6} + \\frac{1}{12}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(d = 1\\).\n\n### Final Calculation\nNow, we calculate \\(a + 2b + 3c + 4d\\):\n\\[\na + 2b + 3c + 4d = 1 + 2(1) + 3(0) + 4(1) = 1 + 2 + 0 + 4 = 7\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{7}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the given problem, we need to analyze the graph of the function \\(\\frac{x^3 - x^2 + x}{6x^2 - 9x}\\). We will find the number of holes, vertical asymptotes, horizontal asymptotes, and oblique asymptotes to determine the values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(c\\), and \\(d\\).\n\nFirst, let's simplify the given function:\n\\[\nf(x) = \\frac{x^3 - x^2 + x}{6x^2 - 9x}\n\\]\n\nFactor the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\nx^3 - x^2 + x = x(x^2 - x + 1)\n\\]\n\\[\n6x^2 - 9x = 3x(2x - 3)\n\\]\n\nThus, the function becomes:\n\\[\nf(x) = \\frac{x(x^2 - x + 1)}{3x(2x - 3)}\n\\]\n\nWe can cancel the common factor of \\(x\\) from the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\nf(x) = \\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{3(2x - 3)} \\quad \\text{for} \\quad x \\neq 0\n\\]\n\n### Holes\nThe original function had a common factor of \\(x\\) in the numerator and the denominator, which was canceled. This indicates a hole at \\(x = 0\\).\n\nThus, \\(a = 1\\).\n\n### Vertical Asymptotes\nVertical asymptotes occur where the denominator of the simplified function is zero, and the numerator is non-zero. Set the denominator \\(3(2x - 3) = 0\\):\n\\[\n2x - 3 = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, there is a vertical asymptote at \\(x = \\frac{3}{2}\\).\n\nThus, \\(b = 1\\).\n\n### Horizontal Asymptotes\nTo find horizontal asymptotes, we compare the degrees of the numerator and the denominator of the simplified function \\(\\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{3(2x - 3)}\\).\n\nThe degree of the numerator \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) is 2.\nThe degree of the denominator \\(3(2x - 3)\\) is 1.\n\nSince the degree of the numerator is greater than the degree of the denominator, there is no horizontal asymptote.\n\nThus, \\(c = 0\\).\n\n### Oblique Asymptotes\nOblique asymptotes occur when the degree of the numerator is exactly one more than the degree of the denominator. Here, the degree of the numerator (2) is exactly one more than the degree of the denominator (1).\n\nTo find the oblique asymptote, we perform polynomial long division of \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) by \\(6x - 9\\).\n\nDivide \\(x^2\\) by \\(6x\\) to get \\(\\frac{x}{6}\\).\n\nMultiply \\(\\frac{x}{6}\\) by \\(6x - 9\\) to get \\(x^2 - \\frac{9x}{6} = x^2 - 1.5x\\).\n\nSubtract \\(x^2 - 1.5x\\) from \\(x^2 - x + 1\\):\n\\[\n(x^2 - x + 1) - (x^2 - 1.5x) = 0.5x + 1\n\\]\n\nDivide \\(0.5x\\) by \\(6x\\) to get \\(\\frac{0.5}{6} = \\frac{1}{12}\\).\n\nMultiply \\(\\frac{1}{12}\\) by \\(6x - 9\\) to get \\(0.5x - 0.75\\).\n\nSubtract \\(0.5x - 0.75\\) from \\(0.5x + 1\\):\n\\[\n(0.5x + 1) - (0.5x - 0.75) = 1.75\n\\]\n\nThus, the quotient is \\(\\frac{x}{6} + \\frac{1}{12}\\), and the remainder is 1.75. Therefore, the oblique asymptote is:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{x}{6} + \\frac{1}{12}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(d = 1\\).\n\n### Final Calculation\nNow, we calculate \\(a + 2b + 3c + 4d\\):\n\\[\na + 2b + 3c + 4d = 1 + 2(1) + 3(0) + 4(1) = 1 + 2 + 0 + 4 = 7\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:881"}
